238
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:50,133
Ah.

239
00:10:50,167 --> 00:10:52,367
So the credits are just gonna
get tinier and tinier

240
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:54,167
until the movie's over then.

241
00:10:56,033 --> 00:10:58,000
Well, movie, I sure hope
you can justify

242
00:10:58,033 --> 00:11:00,300
all this personnel in the film.

243
00:11:00,333 --> 00:11:02,500
Stupid universe,
quit expanding!

244
00:11:04,233 --> 00:11:07,033
Attack of
the killer peanut brittle.

245
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:10,167
Wow. Big.

246
00:11:12,533 --> 00:11:14,500
Hey, the same guy
who wrote the messiah.

247
00:11:14,533 --> 00:11:17,167
Fred gebhardt wrote
the messiah?

248
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:18,967
No, the name before--
handel--

249
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,033
[scoffs]
Never mind.

250
00:11:21,067 --> 00:11:24,033
No. Come on.
You're just recycling names
at this point, movie.

251
00:11:24,067 --> 00:11:26,467
I guess focus groups
demanded more credits.

252
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:28,467
What happened to the fluffy,
absorbent credits?

253
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:30,700
I liked those.

254
00:11:30,733 --> 00:11:34,300
Today, the moon narrowly missed
hitting a man's eye
like a big pizza pie.

255
00:11:34,333 --> 00:11:37,367
Scientists believe
that's amore.

256
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:40,167
Honey, get your moon model
out of the living room.

257
00:11:42,833 --> 00:11:45,167
They make them use
the doggie door.

258
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,467
Sir, I've been testing
the pressure equipment
for the mars project.

259
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:51,133
Forget about that now, chapman.
You leave tomorrow morning.

260
00:11:51,167 --> 00:11:53,500
The general wants someone
with your experience.

261
00:11:53,533 --> 00:11:55,867
- I don't know whether
to feel flattered or not.
- Don't.

262
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:57,533
What exactly
will I be looking for?

263
00:11:57,567 --> 00:12:00,133
- Uh, floating space monsters?
- [Chuckling]

264
00:12:00,167 --> 00:12:02,467
This is no joke, frank.
I wish it were.

265
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,633
- Don't make me hurt you.
- Sorry, sir.

266
00:12:04,667 --> 00:12:06,867
You know how rumors
move around this base.

267
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:10,333
Well, that's one of the things
we hope that you'll dispel.

268
00:12:10,367 --> 00:12:12,467
- Well, you're the colonel.
- I'm the weird guy.

269
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:13,833
I'll take a crack
at anything once.

270
00:12:13,867 --> 00:12:16,333
I'm glad you said that, soldier.

271
00:12:16,367 --> 00:12:18,667
And that's about
all you'll get, frank--
one crack.

272
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:20,967
All I need, sir.

273
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,667
- ♪♪ [Dramatic soundtrack music]
- Geez! Just blast off,
I guess.

274
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:25,867
Don't wait for a countdown
or anything. Geez!

275
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:28,467
Does this tepid little scene
really deserve...

276
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:32,133
♪♪ [Imitating dramatic
soundtrack music]

277
00:12:32,167 --> 00:12:35,967
18,000 miles out
at 2-7-0 degrees azimuth

278
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,800
and 4-7 degrees ecliptic.

279
00:12:38,833 --> 00:12:41,300
Speed: 4.6 miles per second.

280
00:12:41,333 --> 00:12:42,667
[Singsong voice]
I'm not listening.

281
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:44,800
- Check. Computer guidance on.
- [Button clicks]

282
00:12:44,833 --> 00:12:47,000
- Stability course on.
- [Button clicks]

283
00:12:47,033 --> 00:12:49,533
- Directional rockets port off.
- [Button clicks]

284
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:51,967
- Directional rockets
starboard off.
- [Button clicks]

285
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,367
- Test automatic.
- Check.

286
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:55,967
- Party tape. Check.
- [Equipment beeping]

287
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,967
- That does it, captain.
- Honey.

288
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,133
- You can relax a bit now.
- Sweetie.

289
00:13:00,167 --> 00:13:02,633
Takeoff's always the same.

290
00:13:02,667 --> 00:13:04,967
My heart pounds
like a sledgehammer.

291
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,300
♪♪ [Imitating beginning
of peter gabriel's
"sledgehammer"]

292
00:13:07,333 --> 00:13:09,467
- How about the screen?
- Sorry.

293
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:10,967
[Buttons clicking]

294
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,833
The moon is fudge-dipped.

295
00:13:12,867 --> 00:13:16,633
[Chapman]
Never fails to fascinate me
from up here.

296
00:13:16,667 --> 00:13:18,633
You know, captain,

297
00:13:18,667 --> 00:13:20,333
every year of my life,

298
00:13:20,367 --> 00:13:22,500
- I grow more
and more convinced...
- [Scoffs]

299
00:13:22,533 --> 00:13:25,300
That the wisest and best
is to fix our attention

300
00:13:25,333 --> 00:13:27,833
on the good
and on the beautiful--

301
00:13:27,867 --> 00:13:30,800
- don't hit him.
- ...If you'll just take
the time to look at it.

302
00:13:30,833 --> 00:13:32,833
- You're some guy, makonnen.
- [Chuckles]

303
00:13:32,867 --> 00:13:34,533
[Woman on radio]
Lunar base 1 to pegasus iv.

304
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:44,300
Spaceship. Now with
gentle-glide applicator.

305
00:13:44,333 --> 00:13:47,300
What's our elapsed time
from base?

306
00:13:47,333 --> 00:13:50,300
14 hours, 22 minutes,
and 30 seconds.

307
00:13:50,333 --> 00:13:54,133
You could stay up here
another 14 hours
and still see nothing.

308
00:13:54,167 --> 00:13:56,367
- Even 1400 hours.
- Even 14 million.

309
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,033
That's about all
we can do, though.

310
00:13:58,067 --> 00:14:00,167
Are you sure
we're still on course?

311
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:01,867
- Why?
- We're underwater.

312
00:14:01,900 --> 00:14:03,867
Well, I have an idea.

313
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:06,967
- Turn to
2-7-8 degrees azimuth--
- you're mad!

314
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,867
...0-47 degrees ecliptic.

315
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:10,500
Yes, sir.

316
00:14:10,533 --> 00:14:12,667
Changing course
to 2-7-8 degrees azimuth,

317
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:14,800
- ...hold 4-7 degrees ecliptic.
- [Beeps]

318
00:14:14,833 --> 00:14:17,200
[Imitates beep]

319
00:14:17,233 --> 00:14:19,333
Lansfield tells us
to charter the same course

320
00:14:19,367 --> 00:14:21,167
pegasus iii took.

321
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:23,300
Well, that's fine, but I
don't think we'll get anywhere.

322
00:14:23,333 --> 00:14:25,700
- Knowing us.
- If this strange planet

323
00:14:25,733 --> 00:14:27,467
dashes about
like it's supposed to,

324
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:29,867
it figures we won't find it
around here,

325
00:14:29,900 --> 00:14:32,533
so we're changing course.

326
00:14:32,567 --> 00:14:34,633
Lightning never strikes twice
in the same place.

327
00:14:34,667 --> 00:14:36,800
Precisely. You with me?

328
00:14:36,833 --> 00:14:38,833
- Like I said,
you're the captain.
- Unfortunately.

329
00:14:38,867 --> 00:14:41,167
- I like the new reading.
- Thanks, ray.

330
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:42,800
You know, captain,
every year,

331
00:14:42,833 --> 00:14:44,300
I think more and more
of the goodest

332
00:14:44,333 --> 00:14:46,367
and the nicest and bestest
that we can be.

333
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:48,300
Yeah, whatever, ray.

334
00:14:48,333 --> 00:14:50,533
Hey, it's an adult rocket ship.

335
00:14:50,567 --> 00:14:52,033
[Chuckling]

336
00:14:53,667 --> 00:14:55,367
- You know, captain--
- shut up, ray!

337
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:58,167
We could go nuts out here
just waiting for something
to happen.

338
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:01,633
Wanna play asteroids?

339
00:15:01,667 --> 00:15:04,367
- You seem pretty much at ease.
- You know, captain--

340
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:06,467
well, I figure it's just
the same as fishing.

341
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:07,833
You gotta be patient
and wait.

342
00:15:07,867 --> 00:15:09,667
Drink lots of beer.

343
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:11,133
[Alarm beeping]

344
00:15:11,167 --> 00:15:12,867
The electrostatic meter's
going haywire.

345
00:15:12,900 --> 00:15:16,633
Lunar base 1 to pegasus iv.
Can you read me? Over.

346
00:15:16,667 --> 00:15:18,800
- Well, have you been published?
- Pegasus iv to lunar base 1.

347
00:15:18,833 --> 00:15:21,200
We read you. Over.

348
00:15:21,233 --> 00:15:23,633
You are completely
off course, pegasus iv.

349
00:15:23,667 --> 00:15:26,633
- Check your position
immediately.
- Or you'll get such a spanking.

350
00:15:26,667 --> 00:15:28,967
- D computer and spectrometer
are out.
- [Beeping continues]

351
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,133
Pegasus iv to lunar base 1.

352
00:15:31,167 --> 00:15:33,133
We are aware
of being off course.

353
00:15:33,167 --> 00:15:35,133
Cannot explain at present.

354
00:15:35,167 --> 00:15:37,333
We're entering
a heavy magnetic field.

355
00:15:37,367 --> 00:15:39,333
Several instruments are out,
and I'm afraid others are going.

356
00:15:39,367 --> 00:15:42,000
- And it's not my fault.
- Give us our exact position.

357
00:15:42,033 --> 00:15:43,633
Over.

358
00:15:43,667 --> 00:15:44,967
Pegasus iv, you are--
[Static]

359
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:46,533
Completely boned.

360
00:15:46,567 --> 00:15:49,500
Cannot hear you, lunar base 1.
Over.

361
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:51,967
- [Static continues]
- He's so pretty.

362
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,800
Changing to manual control.

363
00:15:53,833 --> 00:15:55,467
That's the good
and beautiful thing to do, sir.

364
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:56,533
[Ray]
Meteors!

365
00:15:56,567 --> 00:15:59,300
Ach!

366
00:15:59,333 --> 00:16:01,533
- Someone on the mir flushed.
- Ooh-hoo.

367
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:07,033
You know, captain,
I'm scared!

368
00:16:07,067 --> 00:16:10,500
- Boy, that was close.
- Yeah. And I'm afraid
it isn't over yet.

369
00:16:10,533 --> 00:16:14,300
Roving gangs of meteors
terrorize the galaxy.

370
00:16:14,333 --> 00:16:16,567
Out of the way,
spaceship punk!

371
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:21,000
We're on a semi-closing course
with them.

372
00:16:21,033 --> 00:16:23,467
Screen one's fading fast.
What about
the infrared detector?

373
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,633
- That's out too.
- The only thing we can do

374
00:16:25,667 --> 00:16:28,333
is turn it 9-0 degrees
to the shower's path.

375
00:16:28,367 --> 00:16:30,200
More meteors are coming.
It's our only chance.

376
00:16:30,233 --> 00:16:33,833
That will be
3-1-1 degrees azimuth

377
00:16:33,867 --> 00:16:36,133
and minus 1-2 degrees ecliptic.

378
00:16:36,167 --> 00:16:40,000
Yeah, fine.
Just turn left.

379
00:16:40,033 --> 00:16:41,500
- Here they come.
- Prepare the milk.

380
00:16:41,533 --> 00:16:43,033
This is it.

381
00:16:44,867 --> 00:16:46,800
- [Explosion]
- Doh!

382
00:16:46,833 --> 00:16:49,300
I filled my pants, sir.
In fact, I think I filled
yours too.

383
00:16:49,333 --> 00:16:50,867
[Chuckles]

384
00:16:54,067 --> 00:16:57,367
Suckers! We'll be back!

385
00:16:57,400 --> 00:16:59,867
Looks like the worst is over.

386
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:01,700
I see a lot of spots.

387
00:17:01,733 --> 00:17:03,967
Those are ionized trails.
They'll persist for awhile.

388
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,633
Well, let's hope
there's no stragglers.

389
00:17:06,667 --> 00:17:08,800
With these instruments out,

390
00:17:08,833 --> 00:17:11,133
there won't be any way
to tell if they're coming
from another direction.

391
00:17:11,167 --> 00:17:12,967
Trick now is to get back
to the moon.

392
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,200
Permission to talk
in flowery prose again, sir?

393
00:17:15,233 --> 00:17:17,200
- I think we'll make it.
- Yeah.

394
00:17:17,233 --> 00:17:19,467
I know you think that.

395
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:21,300
Okay, ray, let's go through
a type-b check.

396
00:17:21,333 --> 00:17:23,033
- All right.
- Hop up on the table.

397
00:17:23,067 --> 00:17:25,800
- Cabin pressure.
- 20, coming up.

398
00:17:25,833 --> 00:17:29,133
- Retro port.
- 20 port, sir.

399
00:17:29,167 --> 00:17:32,133
- Positive.
- Retro starboard.

400
00:17:32,167 --> 00:17:34,467
- Positive.
- Main circuit one.

401
00:17:34,500 --> 00:17:37,867
- Good and beautiful.
- Check.

402
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:39,800
- Negative.
- He looks so hurt.

403
00:17:39,833 --> 00:17:42,967
- Main two.
- Negative.

404
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,300
- How can he say that to me?
- Let's try the auxiliaries.

405
00:17:47,333 --> 00:17:50,300
- Main one.
- Negative.

406
00:17:50,333 --> 00:17:53,467
- Main two.
- Cool, huh,
watching them check things

407
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:56,033
of which we don't know
what they are?

408
00:17:57,367 --> 00:17:59,300
It's not in the circuits?

409
00:17:59,333 --> 00:18:01,800
No. The meteors must've smacked
into the propulsion elements.

410
00:18:01,833 --> 00:18:05,367
- The feed lines?
- Yeah, if we're lucky.

411
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:08,533
Well, there's only one way
to find out.

412
00:18:08,567 --> 00:18:11,667
- Looks like it.
- Yeah. Strip down, oil up.

413
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,633
- You know--
- oh, boy. What?

414
00:18:16,667 --> 00:18:18,633
- ...there's one thing sure.
- What's that?

415
00:18:18,667 --> 00:18:20,500
They knew what they were doing
when they forced us

416
00:18:20,533 --> 00:18:22,967
to go through those space drills
dozens of times.

417
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,133
If I remember correctly,
you had a different opinion.

418
00:18:25,167 --> 00:18:26,967
- [Chuckles]
- Don't rub it in.

419
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,500
That was a bad and unbeautiful
thing to say, sir.

420
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:32,967
Would it be really
that much of a problem

421
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,700
to have human-sized doors?

422
00:18:35,733 --> 00:18:38,167
Lipstick of the gods.

423
00:18:43,200 --> 00:18:45,133
Man, when he starts talking
about his stamp collection,

424
00:18:45,167 --> 00:18:48,500
I just gotta get outside.

425
00:18:48,533 --> 00:18:51,233
Look. There's the problem.
The dryer's leaking.

426
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:56,833
In or out, kids? What, are we
heating the whole universe?

427
00:18:58,533 --> 00:19:01,300
I gotta get the model number
for the repairman.

428
00:19:01,333 --> 00:19:05,533
I miss the earth so much.
I miss my wife.

429
00:19:05,567 --> 00:19:08,867
Uh, could you get your butt
out of my face, ron?

430
00:19:10,667 --> 00:19:13,033
Good thing there's
so much gravity out in space.

431
00:19:13,067 --> 00:19:14,467
♪♪ [Ambient soundtrack music]

432
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:17,233
Soundtrack by percy faith
and his orchestra.

433
00:19:19,533 --> 00:19:21,833
Hmm, that towel we stuffed
in that crack

434
00:19:21,867 --> 00:19:24,300
really isn't doing the trick.

435
00:19:24,333 --> 00:19:26,000
I can't believe you left
your tether ropes

436
00:19:26,033 --> 00:19:28,500
at lloyd's cabin.

437
00:19:28,533 --> 00:19:30,500
Um, I'm not good at this, sir.

438
00:19:30,533 --> 00:19:33,667
I'm only good at fruity,
philosophical speeches.

439
00:19:36,233 --> 00:19:40,533
Don't move, ron.
There are two giant eyeballs
on your back.

440
00:19:40,567 --> 00:19:44,067
Ahh. You smell good, sir.
Like vitalis and bacon.

441
00:19:47,667 --> 00:19:50,133
Doh-ho-ho!
Doh, stanley!

442
00:19:50,167 --> 00:19:52,133
It's a sperm whale-powered ship,
sir.

443
00:19:52,167 --> 00:19:53,633
You have to watch out
for his glow-hole.

444
00:19:53,667 --> 00:19:55,133
[Laughter]

445
00:19:55,167 --> 00:19:57,033
You're terrible astronauts.

446
00:19:59,833 --> 00:20:02,167
Oh, look.
It's the interior of our rocket.

447
00:20:02,200 --> 00:20:04,700
Cool. You can see
where we sit.

448
00:20:04,733 --> 00:20:06,967
This looks like it.

449
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,233
The retro rocket feed lines
must be cut.

450
00:20:11,833 --> 00:20:14,500
The guy on the left
has a curvy puckeredesque butt.

451
00:20:16,500 --> 00:20:19,800
Mosquitoes are bad tonight.

452
00:20:19,833 --> 00:20:23,300
Yeah, it used to be great
when you could fix
those rockets yourself

453
00:20:23,333 --> 00:20:25,967
with just a crescent wrench
before all that computer stuff.

454
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:27,000
Yeah.

455
00:20:29,067 --> 00:20:31,467
Frank, your air line's broken.
Frank!

456
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:34,133
- ♪ Motion in the ocean ♪
- ♪ ewww ♪

457
00:20:34,167 --> 00:20:36,633
- ♪ his air hose broke ♪
- ♪ ew, oh ♪

458
00:20:36,667 --> 00:20:38,667
Come on, captain load.

459
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:40,533
[Chuckles]

460
00:20:43,033 --> 00:20:45,000
[Mike, as if over radio]
I'm coming, elizabeth.
[Static]

461
00:20:45,033 --> 00:20:47,167
Elizabeth, I'm coming.
Over.

462
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:49,000
It's black bart and his gang.

463
00:20:49,033 --> 00:20:51,967
Get away from that rocket,
ya varmints!

464
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,633
Okay. Break.
Back to position one.

465
00:20:53,667 --> 00:20:55,867
And wrestle.

466
00:20:55,900 --> 00:20:57,867
Since you're gonna
die anyway, ron,

467
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,233
I'm gonna stick you
right in the meat locker, okay?

468
00:21:03,167 --> 00:21:07,333
Uhh! Uhh!
Well, I'm glad you're safe, sir,
but could you lend me--

469
00:21:07,367 --> 00:21:10,200
oh, never mind. I'm fine.
It's okay. I'll handle it.

470
00:21:10,233 --> 00:21:13,033
Yep. Just got to, uh--
I'm fine.

471
00:21:13,067 --> 00:21:15,033
If you could just prop
the door open--

472
00:21:15,067 --> 00:21:17,667
nope. That's-- that's fine.

473
00:21:17,700 --> 00:21:21,133
Could you just give a message
to my wife and kids,
if you have the ti--

474
00:21:21,167 --> 00:21:23,667
well, never mind.

475
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:28,633
Our father who art in heaven,

476
00:21:28,667 --> 00:21:30,700
hallowed by thy name...

477
